  "body": "## Scope\n\nThis PR is intentionally based on restored stable `91658cc4` and changes only session/browser-auth safety. It does **not** port later category/filter behavior or offer/negotiation feature changes.\n\n## Confirmed failure paths in 91658cc4\n\n1. A stuck/slow `/session-refresh` could trigger a clean-session recovery that cleared cookies, localStorage and sessionStorage.\n2. Login could treat the mere absence of known login controls as proof of authentication.\n3. The pre-job checkpoint saved `bot-X.json` immediately after login returned, without a second strong-auth guard.\n4. On an unsafe job ending, code restored `last-known-good`, which is actually the session from *before* the fresh pre-job checkpoint and can contain older auth tokens.\n5. Headless mode blocked Vinted service workers by default.\n6. Price-probe execution could share the production session context.\n7. Two independently launched Playwright JVMs could write the same session files concurrently.\n\n## Fix\n\n- Never clear cookies/storage or invalidate persisted state because session-refresh/navigation is merely slow.\n- Require visible conversations/inbox evidence before treating login as authenticated and before replacing `bot-X.json`.\n- Give a restored session a bounded ~8s UI settle window before interactive login.\n- Keep the fresh authenticated pre-job checkpoint if a job fails or finishes on an unsafe auth page; do not roll back to the older rotating backup.\n- Keep service workers enabled by default; explicit env override can still block them.\n- Isolate PRICE_PROBE from production session restore/persistence.\n- Fail closed when production storageState cannot be restored.\n- Add an OS-level runtime lock for the shared session directory.\n- Add regression tests for refresh preservation, strong auth evidence, restored-session settling, checkpoint retention, service-worker default and duplicate runtime ownership.\n\n## Session files\n\nActive sessions remain `sessions/bot-X.json`. `.bot-*.json.tmp` files are staging files and stale ones are cleaned on startup. `sessions/backups` remains a recovery safety net and should not be manually deleted during normal operation.",
